items/prop-qftx-anomaly-obstruction.md:100:5.1 The counterterm class $[b_4]_{\mathscr F_n}$ is admissible by [A2]. Replacing $X$ by $X-\hbar^n[b_4]_{\mathscr F_n}$ changes the order-$n$ breaking by $-s[b_4]_{\mathscr F_n}$, because the leading term of $\mathcal W_\hbar$ is $s$. Step 4.1 gives $[a_4]_{\mathscr F_n}=s[b_4]_{\mathscr F_n}$, since $d_Hc_3$ is zero in that quotient, so the breaking is canceled. [F2, A1, A2, step 4.1, algebra]
items/prop-qftx-anomaly-obstruction.md:102:6.1 Conversely, if an admissible local counterterm class $[b_4]_{\mathscr F_n}$ cancels the order-$n$ breaking in $\mathscr F_n^1$, then $[a_4]_{\mathscr F_n}-s[b_4]_{\mathscr F_n}=0$. By the quotient definition in [F2], $a_4-sb_4=d_Hc_3$ for some $c_3\in\mathcal K_n^{1,3}$. This is exactly the relative coboundary condition; step 3.1 ensures that $[a_4]_{s|d_H}$ is the class under discussion. [F2, A1, A2, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
items/def-qftx-wilsonian-n1-action-data.md:68:A regulated formal expectation $\langle\mathcal O\rangle_\lambda$ is the normalized coefficientwise functional assigned by a specified cutoff prescription; the notation asserts no convergence or physical continuum measure. A local insertion $\mathcal O$ is a polynomial or formal local function of the fields and finitely many spacetime derivatives, multiplied by a compactly supported test function. For an insertion $\mathcal O$ and the rigid supersymmetry variation generated by the N=1 supercharges, its Ward identity is the change-of-variables equation $\langle\delta_{\epsilon,\bar\epsilon}\mathcal O\rangle_\lambda=\langle\mathcal O\,\delta_{\epsilon,\bar\epsilon}S_\lambda\rangle_\lambda$. The BRST Ward identity is $\langle s\mathcal O\rangle_\lambda=0$ when $sS_\lambda=0$. A local gauge anomaly is a nonzero local insertion in the latter identity. A regulator preserves these Ward identities when the equations hold coefficientwise at every perturbative order without an anomaly insertion; this definition does not assert that such a regulator exists for every field representation.
items/def-qftx-wilsonian-n1-action-data.md:70:The spurion argument additionally uses the local background extension of this formal prescription: independent F coefficients and $\tau$ are nondynamical chiral superfields, and independent real D coefficients are unconstrained real superfields. The prescribed regulated counterterms obey the same superspace and gauge Ward identities for these backgrounds and have a local derivative expansion in them. The derivative-free chiral sector is evaluated at constant scalar backgrounds after that extension; background-superderivative terms belong to the real D sector after chiral projection. This is the usual spurion-compatible regulator hypothesis, not a consequence of classical BRST nilpotence or a regulator-existence theorem. Its counterterms depend on the original background coefficients, so a redundant parametrization of those coefficients introduces no independent physical coupling. No rescaling of an integrated matter or vector field is assumed in this extension.
items/def-qftx-wilsonian-n1-action-data.md:72:The component background fields are smooth on $M$, and admissible variations and insertions are compactly supported or satisfy decay conditions that make the stated total-derivative boundary terms vanish. Loop momenta are regulated separately by the Wilsonian high-mode prescription.
