items/thm-aqft-finite-group-gauging.md:38:Let $K=K_1\cup_\Sigma K_2$ be a finite ordered simplicial complex written as a union of two finite subcomplexes with common subcomplex $\Sigma$. Let $\mathcal F$, $\mathcal F_1$, $\mathcal F_2$, and $\mathcal S$ be finite background field groupoids for $K$, $K_1$, $K_2$, and $\Sigma$, with restriction functors $r_i:\mathcal F_i\to\mathcal S$. The homotopy pullback $\mathcal H=\mathcal F_2\times^h_{\mathcal S}\mathcal F_1$ has objects $(x_2,x_1,\alpha)$ with $\alpha:r_2(x_2)\overset{\cong}{\longrightarrow}r_1(x_1)$ and morphisms $(u_2,u_1)$ satisfying $r_1(u_1)\circ\alpha=\alpha'\circ r_2(u_2)$.
items/thm-aqft-finite-group-gauging.md:62:[F1] [[def-aqft-background-gauging-data]]: a finite background field groupoid retains its automorphism groups; a cocycle/action weight is constant on every arrow; its gauging sum is the sum over isomorphism classes weighted by $1/|\operatorname{Aut}|$.
items/prop-qftx-free-euclidean-reflection-positive.md:122:3.1 Define $h_f(\mathbf k)=\int_0^\infty e^{-\omega_{\mathbf k}t}\widehat f(t,\mathbf k)\,dt$ and use the measure $d\nu_m(\mathbf k)=d\mathbf k/((2\pi)^{d-1}2\omega_{\mathbf k})$, with the one-point convention from the Statement when $d=1$. Compact time support and the rapid spatial decay just proved give $h_f\in L^2(\nu_m)$, and step 2.2 gives $C_m(\overline{f\circ\vartheta},g)=\langle h_f,h_g\rangle_{L^2(\nu_m)}$, where $\langle u,v\rangle_{L^2(\nu_m)}:=\int\overline{u(\mathbf k)}v(\mathbf k)\,d\nu_m(\mathbf k)$ is linear in its second argument. To pass from this Gram form to the full Gaussian reflection form, fix any finite set of positive-time tests $f_1,\ldots,f_N$ used by $a$. Since all $S_n$ are symmetric, their moment functional on this finite set of field variables factors through its commutative polynomial algebra. Write $X(f_i)$ for the degree-one generator $f_i$ in $T_d$. The formal Wick exponential $W(z)=\exp\!\left(\sum_i z_iX(f_i)-\frac12\sum_{i,j}z_iz_jC_m(f_i,f_j)\right)$ has polynomial coefficients; no analytic convergence is asserted. Write $s_i^\#$ for the formal conjugate of $s_i$: reflection conjugates coefficients, so $\Theta W(s)$ uses $s_i^\#$ and conjugates the coefficients of the quadratic counterterm. Put $G_{ij}=C_m(f_i,f_j)$ and $R_{ij}=C_m(\overline{f_i\circ\vartheta},f_j)$. Applying [F5] to the joint finite real list consisting of the real and imaginary parts of $f_i$ and $\overline{f_i\circ\vartheta}$ gives, by complex multilinearity, the coefficientwise formal identity. Each coefficient on the right is the sum over pairings of the corresponding finite test list: the quadratic exponential chooses unordered pairs, and its factorial denominator cancels their ordering multiplicity. Thus the identity follows coefficient by coefficient, with no convergence of an infinite series asserted. $\omega_S\!\left(\exp\!\left(\sum_i s_i^\#X(\overline{f_i\circ\vartheta})\right)\exp\!\left(\sum_j z_jX(f_j)\right)\right) =\exp\!\left(\frac12\sum_{i,j}s_i^\#\overline{G_{ij}}s_j^\#+\sum_{i,j}s_i^\#R_{ij}z_j+\frac12\sum_{i,j}z_iG_{ij}z_j\right).$ The identity $C_m(\overline{f_i\circ\vartheta},\overline{f_j\circ\vartheta})=\overline{G_{ij}}$ for the reflected covariance follows from $\widehat{\overline{f\circ\vartheta}}(p_0,\mathbf k)=\overline{\widehat f(p_0,-\mathbf k)}$: substitute this in the Fourier formula for $C_m$ and change $\mathbf k$ to $-\mathbf k$, using the real multiplier $p_0^2+|\mathbf k|^2+m^2$. Multiplying by the two Wick counterterms therefore cancels the first and third quadratic sums and leaves $\omega_S(\Theta W(s)\,W(z))=\exp\!\left(\sum_{i,j}s_i^\#C_m(\overline{f_i\circ\vartheta},f_j)z_j\right),$ where $s$ and $z$ are independent formal variables and the symbol $s_i^\#$ is conjugated when coefficients are evaluated. The coefficient of unequal degrees in $s^\#,z$ is zero. Differentiating $k$ times in each variable gives the cross-pairing matrix $\sum_{\sigma\in\Sigma_k}\prod_{j=1}^kR_{i_j\ell_{\sigma(j)}}$, where $R_{i\ell}=\langle h_{f_i},h_{f_\ell}\rangle$. Write the finite Wick expansion of a polynomial as $a=\sum_{k=0}^K\sum_{i_1,\ldots,i_k}a^{(k)}_{i_1\ldots i_k}\partial_{z_{i_1}}\cdots\partial_{z_{i_k}}W(0)$, with symmetric coefficient tensors, and let $A_k=\sum a^{(k)}_{i_1\ldots i_k}e_{i_1}\otimes\cdots\otimes e_{i_k}$. The triangular relation between ordinary monomials and Wick polynomials, whose leading terms agree and whose remaining terms have lower degree, proves that every finite polynomial has such a finite expansion. Let $H:\mathbb C^N\to L^2(\nu_m)$ be the linear map $He_i=h_{f_i}$, and let $\operatorname{Sym}$ be the average over the $k!$ permutations of tensor factors. Expanding the squared norm of $\operatorname{Sym}H^{\otimes k}A_k$ gives $1/k!$ times the sum over those permutations, so the displayed cross-pairing matrix makes the degree-$k$ reflected quadratic form exactly $k!\|\operatorname{Sym}H^{\otimes k}A_k\|^2\ge0$. There are no cross terms between different degrees. Therefore for every $a\in T_{d,+}$, $\omega_S(\Theta a\,a)$ is a finite sum of nonnegative squared norms. [F1, F2, F5, step 2.1, step 2.2, given, algebra] ∎ 
