items/thm-qftx-scalar-eg-induction.md:129:[F8] [[prop-qft-local-counterterm-jet-classification]]: same-degree extensions differ by a finite sum of $\partial^\alpha\delta_0$ with $|\alpha|\le\lfloor\delta-d\rfloor$ for $\delta\ge d$, and agree uniquely for $\delta<d$. The same point-support proof bounds the difference of two upper-degree-$B$ extensions by order $\lfloor B-d\rfloor$, since a nonzero point-supported sum of largest order $k$ has degree $d+k$.
items/thm-qftx-scalar-eg-induction.md:167:8.1 For the finite block in step 7.2 choose a common finite engineering upper bound $B$ compatible with its homogeneous label degrees and the finitely many lower-order counterterms. Linear coordinate changes commute with positive dilations and preserve these bounds. Put $J_N=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb C}\{\partial^\alpha\delta_0:|\alpha|\le N\}$, where $N=\lfloor B-d\rfloor$ when $B\ge d$, and put $J_N=0$ when $B<d$. The permitted local corrections form the invariant subspace $V$ of $\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbb C}(L,J_N\otimes O)$ satisfying the labelwise engineering bounds and the linear identities of the balanced coefficients. This space is finite-dimensional. Lorentz substitutions preserve each homogeneous degree and delta-derivative order, while [F11] makes the derivative identities equivariant, proving invariance of this subspace. Differentiate the combined action to obtain $\rho(X)$ and put $c(X)=\rho(X)t^1$. Restriction commutes with the action, and step 7.2 gives $\rho(X)t^0=0$, so $c(X)$ is supported at the relative origin. It obeys the same engineering bounds and identities; [F8] and [F10] therefore place it in $V$. Smooth differentiation is justified by differentiating the transformed compact test densities in their fixed-support smooth topology; the finite-order distribution bound then permits pairing. The representation law gives $c([X,Y])=\rho(X)c(Y)-\rho(Y)c(X)$, so $c:\mathfrak{so}(1,3)\to V$ is exactly a real-linear cocycle in the finite-dimensional complex module required by [F9]. [F8, F9, F10, F11, step 7.2, algebra]
items/thm-qftx-scalar-eg-induction.md:179:The result is conditional on the fixed free scalar data in the definition. It constructs formal local time-ordered products and finite counterterms; it does not construct the free data, prove convergence, take an adiabatic limit, or impose a gauge identity.
