items/def-qftx-formal-perturbative-yang-mills-data.md:11:axiom_base: "ZF + AC_omega inherited through the selected pure SU(2) bundle model; the supplied formal counterterm sequence requires no additional choice."
items/def-qftx-formal-perturbative-yang-mills-data.md:41:      locator: "§§2.2–2.3 and 12.2–12.3: Yang–Mills-type local BRST fields, local functionals modulo total derivatives, and the cohomological roles of counterterms and anomalies."
items/def-qftx-formal-perturbative-yang-mills-data.md:59:A **formal perturbative renormalization datum** consists of the selected pure SU(2) classical BV action $\Gamma_0$, the above gauge-fixing choice, the dimensional-regularization prescription $D=4-2\epsilon$, modified minimal subtraction of the $1/\epsilon$ poles together with $-\gamma_E+\log(4\pi)$, and a local counterterm space $\mathcal C_{\leq d_*}$. Here $d_*$ is a finite engineering-dimension bound fixed by the selected power-counting prescription; $\mathcal C_{\leq d_*}$ consists of integrated Lorentz-scalar polynomials of ghost number zero and engineering dimension at most $d_*$ in $A,c,\bar c,b$, their antifields, and finitely many spacetime derivatives. The usual renormalizable four-dimensional Yang–Mills choice is $d_*=4$. The minimal BV action is the extension of $S_0$ by the antifield couplings to $sA_\mu=D_\mu c$ and $sc=-\tfrac12[c,c]$; when the nonminimal pair is included it also has the term $-\int_U\bar c^*b$. The datum is admissible only when $\Gamma_0$ satisfies the classical master equation and the chosen local BV functional domain has no nonzero central element of ghost number one. Its loop-counting variable $\lambda$ is formal and dimensionless; a formal effective action is a coefficient sequence $\Gamma(\lambda)=\Gamma_0+\sum_{n\geq1}\lambda^n\Gamma_n$ and no convergence at any nonzero $\lambda$ is asserted.
items/def-qftx-susy-background-data.md:66:A rigid supersymmetric background datum is a fixed Riemannian metric, spin structure and spin connection, R-symmetry background, supergravity auxiliary background fields, and a nonzero smooth spinor section $\epsilon$ in a declared spinor/R-symmetry representation. It also supplies the exact first-order linear system $\mathscr K(\nabla^S,A_R,\mathcal B)(\epsilon)=0$ imposed on $\epsilon$, where $\mathcal B$ denotes the named auxiliary background fields and $\mathscr K$ is the operator obtained by setting the chosen supergravity fermion variation to zero. The equation, its signature, gamma-matrix convention, spinor reality conditions, and boundary conditions must be specified separately for each model; no universal Killing-spinor equation or existence statement is built into this definition. “Rigid” means the metric and supergravity fields are fixed external data and are not integrated over.
items/def-qftx-susy-background-data.md:68:An off-shell multiplet is a specified $\mathbb Z_2$-graded collection of bosonic, fermionic, and auxiliary fields together with an odd transformation $Q$ on all of them. An isometry is a diffeomorphism preserving $g$ and $o$; its action on associated bundles must also be supplied. A flavor transformation is the action of a separately named global internal group on the declared field representations. The multiplet closes off shell when $Q^2$ equals an explicitly declared combination of lifted spacetime isometries, gauge transformations, R-symmetry transformations, and flavor transformations on every field, without using Euler–Lagrange equations. A localization package additionally specifies its field configuration space $\mathcal F$, action $S$, gauge group $\mathcal G$, a gauge-fixing slice (a locally transverse condition on gauge orbits), the associated Faddeev–Popov operator and determinant, and a ghost complex (graded ghost, antighost, and multiplier fields with an odd BRST differential). A Faddeev–Popov zero mode is a vector in the kernel of the specified gauge-fixing operator; stabilizer modes are infinitesimal gauge transformations that fix the chosen connection. The package also specifies its integration contour $\mathcal C$, boundary conditions, and either an actual measure or a regulator map from field configurations to regulated spaces $\mathcal F_\Lambda$ with measures $\mu_\Lambda$. If a measure is only written formally as $[D\Phi]$, this notation is not a constructed measure. Applying a $Q$-exact deformation $S_t=S+tQV$ further requires an explicit assumption or proof of $Q$-invariance of the regulated measure and contour, absence or treatment of anomalies, justified differentiation under the integral, and vanishing or controlled boundary terms. This definition supplies no such proof.
items/def-qftx-brst-relative-local-cohomology.md:36:    - title: Rudolf Schmid, Local cohomology in gauge theories, BRST transformations and anomalies
items/def-qftx-local-time-ordered-system.md:81:Field independence means that each numerical graph distribution is independent of the background field $\phi$; all $\phi$-dependence enters through the vertex kernels $F_i^{(r)}(\phi)$. In the graph expansion of $T_n$, vertices are labeled by $1,\ldots,n$ and an edge between distinct vertices contracts one derivative slot at each endpoint by $H_F$. A graph is a finite loopless multigraph, so parallel edges are allowed; if $r_{ij}$ edges join vertices $i$ and $j$, the Wick coefficient for that choice includes $\hbar^{\sum_{i<j}r_{ij}}/\prod_{i<j}r_{ij}!$. Finite-order local jet derivatives may act on incident edge kernels. Each coefficient is a finite sum of such contractions, since every input has finite polynomial degree. No graph term uses a singular product at a diagonal before extension.
items/def-qftx-local-time-ordered-system.md:85:For a graph containing proper subgraphs, use a finite upper bound obtained by causal block induction from the edge degrees and the finite derivative orders of the chosen lower-order local counterterms; total derivatives add their orders to that bound. Any two extensions of a fixed punctured coefficient with upper bound $B_\Gamma$ differ by derivatives $\partial^\beta\delta_0$ of order $|\beta|\le\lfloor B_\Gamma-4(n-1)\rfloor$ when $B_\Gamma\ge4(n-1)$, and agree when $B_\Gamma<4(n-1)$. The allowed counterterms must also satisfy the stated linearity and derivative identities. Precisely, for an independent balanced punctured coefficient $t\in\mathcal D\prime(\mathbb R^d\setminus\{0\})$, $d=4(n-1)$, of actual degree $\delta=\operatorname{sd}_0(t)\in\mathbb R$, its degree-preserving extension family is $\mathcal E_\delta(t)=\{T\in\mathcal D\prime(\mathbb R^d):T|_{\mathbb R^d\setminus\{0\}}=t,\ \operatorname{sd}_0(T)=\delta\}$. For this family, $\delta$ replaces $B_\Gamma$ in the ambiguity bound. If $\operatorname{sd}_0(t)=-\infty$, degree preservation means $\operatorname{sd}_0(T)=-\infty$ and the ambiguity is zero. The analytic extension stage means this distribution-theoretic extension step; the contract imposes no additional dependence on a complex regularization parameter. A differentiated coefficient is not required to retain the exact scaling degree of a punctured linear combination that vanishes: total differentiation may force a contact term at the diagonal.
items/def-aqft-two-group-symmetry-realization.md:28:physical_scope: Finite-regulator Euclidean models on closed oriented triangulated d-manifolds with finite discrete 2-group backgrounds. This is a model-data interface; it asserts no QFT realization, anomaly absence, continuum limit, or empirical result.
