items/def-aqft-two-group-symmetry-realization.md:63:Thus the two background fields are constrained and are not independent. A $1$-gauge arrow is given by vertex labels $g_i\in G$ and edge labels $\lambda_{ij}\in A$, with
items/def-aqft-two-group-symmetry-realization.md:77:A permitted background counterterm is a model-declared dimensionless functional $L_x\in\mathbb R/(2\pi\mathbb Z)$ that is a finite sum of functions, each depending only on cochain labels in one simplex or a specified finite cluster of simplices; it reweights the model by $e^{iL_x}$. If under a gauge arrow $f:x\to y$ all unnormalized correlators transform by a common phase $\chi_f(x)\in U(1)$, coherence requires $\chi_{\mathrm{id}_x}(x)=1$, $\chi_{g\circ f}(x)=\chi_g(y)\chi_f(x)$ for composable arrows, and equal phases for $2$-isomorphic arrows. The residual background anomaly is the class of these phases under $\chi_f(x)\mapsto e^{iL_y}\chi_f(x)e^{-iL_x}$ for the permitted counterterms. The anomaly is absent in the selected scheme exactly when an allowed counterterm makes every such phase $1$. The Postnikov class specifies the background constraint and gauge transformations; it is not itself the model's transformation phase or anomaly. It may enter an anomaly calculation for a specified action and measure.
