items/prop-qftx-finite-renormalization-composition.md:89:4.1 Fix balanced input and remaining-field labels and one $\hbar$ coefficient of the polarized residual in step 3.1, and call its numerical distribution $Q$. Its field-independent numerical coefficients are translation invariant by covariance in [F1]; lower-order counterterm insertions preserve that invariance by the induction hypothesis. In coordinates $y=x_n$, $z_i=x_i-x_n$, simultaneous translation acts only on $y\in\mathbb R^4$, and step 3.1 gives $\operatorname{supp}Q\subseteq\{z=0\}$. Translation invariance implies $Q(\partial_{y_a}\Psi)=0$ for every test $\Psi$, by differentiating its translated test curve in the fixed-support smooth topology. Choose $\eta(y)=\prod_{a=1}^4\eta_a(y_a)$ with $\eta_a\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R)$ and $\int\eta_a=1$, using [F5], and define $q(\psi)=Q(\eta(y)\psi(z))$. For any $\Psi$, replace its dependence on $y_1$, then $y_2,y_3,y_4$, by $\eta_a(y_a)$ times its integral in that coordinate. Each replacement difference has zero integral in that coordinate, so its primitive from $-\infty$ is compactly supported and smooth, also in the remaining variables. Hence $\Psi-\eta(y)\int\Psi(v,z)\,d^4v$ is a sum of four $y$-derivatives of compact smooth tests. The derivative-annihilation identity yields $Q(\Psi)=q(\int\Psi(y,z)\,d^4y)$, that is, $Q=1_y\otimes q$. The map defining $q$ is continuous on every fixed-support test space, and its support is contained in $\{0\}\subset\mathbb R^{4(n-1)}$. [F1, F5, step 2.1, step 3.1, construct, algebra]
