items/lem-qftx-finite-graph-scaling-degree.md:105:5.1 For a primitive graph, the definitions above mean every proper induced vertex subgraph lies below its divergence threshold. Induct on vertex number with the engineering upper bound $b_I=\sum_{e\text{ internal to }I}(2+|\alpha_e|)$ for a block $I$, and likewise for $J$. For independent balanced subgraph coefficients choose same-degree extensions by [F5]; their total-derivative descendants and any allowed local normalizations retain the contract's engineering upper bounds. Thus the lower-order block kernels, including any permitted finite delta-derivative terms, have these nonnegative upper bounds, starting from the zero bound at one vertex. Step 3.1 then counts every edge exactly once and step 4.1 gives $\operatorname{sd}_0(t_\Gamma)\le2|E(\Gamma)|+\sum_e|\alpha_e|$. If a proper vertex subgraph has a chosen local counterterm, [F5] makes it a finite sum of finite-order delta derivatives. Each has finite relative scaling degree equal to its relative dimension plus its derivative order. Its translation-invariant lift still has block momentum sum zero by step 1.1, so steps 2.1–4.1 apply with a finite nonnegative upper bound for that chosen block. There are only finitely many vertex subsets and finitely many counterterm jets at this fixed graph order. Taking their finite maximum proves the stated general finite upper bound. [A1, F2, F5, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, algebra] ∎
