+---
+id: pthm-aqft-symmetry-preserving-rg-anomaly-match
+kind: physics-theorem
+title: "Symmetry-preserving RG anomaly matching"
+status: draft
+origin: pipeline
+pipeline_run: advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23
+domain: physics
+library: physics
+category: advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry
+page: qftx-anomaly-matching-and-frontier
+dependency_level: 4
+deps:
+  - def-aqft-exact-background-rg-flow
+  - def-aqft-thooft-anomaly
+  - def-aqft-global-anomaly-holonomy
+dependency_roles:
+  def-aqft-exact-background-rg-flow: formulation-prerequisite
+  def-aqft-thooft-anomaly: formulation-prerequisite
+  def-aqft-global-anomaly-holonomy: formulation-prerequisite
+proof_strategy: direct
+provenance:
+  statement: literature-derived
+  proof: ai-generated
+physical_scope: "Conditional matching of the selected background-gauge anomaly class, including declared global holonomies, for a finite-regulator exact symmetry-preserving RG comparison on a fixed closed Euclidean spacetime. No continuum limit, phase exclusion, spectrum claim, or empirical result is asserted."
+sources:
+  references:
+    - title: "Jeffrey A. Harvey, TASI 2003 Lectures on Anomalies"
+      url: "https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0509097"
+      locator: "§2.3, 't Hooft matching conditions: an unbroken global symmetry's anomaly must be reproduced in the infrared; the argument uses background gauging and spectator fermions."
+    - title: "David Tong, Gauge Theory, full lecture notes"
+      url: "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/gauge-theory/gauge.pdf"
+      locator: "§3.5, printed pp. 176–178, for the spectator-fermion anomaly-matching argument and equality of UV/IR anomaly coefficients when the symmetry survives; §5.6.1 for a conditional QCD application."
+verification:
+  precheck: pending
+---
+
+## Statement
+
+Use natural units $c=\hbar=1$ and dimensionless Euclidean generating functionals. Fix a selected finite-regulator model record on a compact closed oriented Euclidean $d$-manifold with its declared tangential structure and boundary conditions, including the actual field multiplets, representations, regulator, and measure at every scale. Fix a compact internal global symmetry group $G$ with its actual global form and a background groupoid with the same admitted nondynamical backgrounds and gauge arrows at both scales. The symmetry is exact and unbroken along the flow and is not a dynamical gauge redundancy. On each common nonzero background patch, let $W_\mu[b]$ be a local branch of the logarithm of the partition functional and set $a_\mu(g;b)=W_\mu[b^g]-W_\mu[b]$ for every admitted arrow $g:b\to b^g$; transition phases on overlapping patches record the global holonomies. Define the selected anomaly class to be the equivalence class of these variations and holonomies modulo variations of the declared single-valued local background counterterms.
+
+Suppose the exact Wilsonian comparison from $\mu_{\mathrm{UV}}$ to $\mu_{\mathrm{IR}}$ retains all remaining low-energy and topological sectors and satisfies $W_{\mu_{\mathrm{UV}}}[b]-W_{\mu_{\mathrm{IR}}}[b]=C[b]$ for one admissible local counterterm $C$ on the full background domain, in compatible local branches. Then the ultraviolet and complete infrared anomaly classes agree, including their counterterm-equivalence classes of global holonomies. This is a conditional matching constraint only; it does not determine the infrared spectrum, prove a gap, exclude a proposed phase, or establish an interacting continuum QFT.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Harvey, *TASI 2003 Lectures on Anomalies*, §2.3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0509097)
+
+[Tong, *Gauge Theory*, §§3.5 and 5.6.1](https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/gauge-theory/gauge.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The finite-regulator model tuple, natural units, closed spacetime and tangential structure, field content and representations, exact global group and global form, common background groupoid, nonzero local partition branches, and exact RG comparison stated above.
+
+[F1] [[def-aqft-thooft-anomaly]]: the exact planned definition is a background-gauge variation modulo a specified set of local counterterms, with dynamical-gauge inconsistency and explicit symmetry breaking separated. Its carrier is absent from the current batch-41 inventory.
+
+[F2] [[def-aqft-global-anomaly-holonomy]]: the exact planned definition is a phase or holonomy of the partition-function or determinant line around a specified loop in backgrounds modulo gauge transformations, including the tangential structure. Its carrier is absent from the current batch-42 inventory.
+
+[F3] [[def-aqft-exact-background-rg-flow]]: the current local definition fixes the finite-regulator model and scale data, the background groupoid and exact symmetry action, the Wilsonian shell comparison, all retained infrared sectors, and the single-valued local functional $C$ relating the two background responses.
+
+[A1] Every RG step acts equivariantly on the same allowed background groupoid and uses the selected model's declared global $G$-action, field representations, regulator, measure, and boundary conditions; no transformation is removed from the anomaly test.
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 Fix an admitted arrow $g:b\to b'$. In a common nonzero local trivialization, substitute the exact flow relation at $b'$ and $b$ into the background variation:
+
+$$
+a_{\mu_{\mathrm{UV}}}(g;b)=W_{\mu_{\mathrm{UV}}}[b']-W_{\mu_{\mathrm{UV}}}[b]=a_{\mu_{\mathrm{IR}}}(g;b)+C[b']-C[b].
+$$
+
+The same identity holds for transition phases on overlaps because the flow relation is compatible on the full background domain. [F3, A1, algebra]
+
+2.1 The difference $C[b']-C[b]$ is exactly the variation of the declared admissible local counterterm $C$. By [F1], the local anomaly cocycles therefore represent the same counterterm-equivalence class. Around any closed loop in the background quotient, the counterterm factors telescope to the endpoint variation of this same $C$; this is precisely the equivalence allowed for the global holonomies in [F2]. Thus every component of the selected anomaly class agrees. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎

